    {
      "calls": [
        {
          "ID": "toolu_01TCdNbDhHWRiJ2ySse8Yg7a",
          "Name": "submit_read",
          "Input": {
            "assessments": [
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S1:F2",
                  "S1:F7",
                  "S2:F6",
                  "S6:F22",
                  "S7:F29",
                  "S6:F21",
                  "F30"
                ],
                "ref": "N1",
                "statement": "In the days after the US\the Russia agreement of 9 October and President Trump's reciprocal energy ceasefire of 11 October, the UK moved to increase rather than ease pressure on Russia and support for Ukraine, with wider oil sanctions, missile deliveries and further packages ordered, which suggests London is not treating the US track as a reason to let up.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S13:F51",
                  "S14:F56",
                  "F57",
                  "F55"
                ],
                "ref": "N2",
                "statement": "The Scottish Government's agreement to match the UK's £10 million for two defence technical excellence colleges is an early, concrete case of the joint working the Prime Minister offered Holyrood, but it is a single co-funded project, not yet a wider settlement on devolved powers.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S8:F31",
                  "S8:F32",
                  "S8:F34",
                  "S9:F35",
                  "S11:F37",
                  "F33"
                ],
                "ref": "N3",
                "statement": "The government is clustering the milestones of its publicly owned railway in Derby (the GBR headquarters, the first apprentice starters and the £1 billion northern fleet at Alstom's site), so rail nationalisation is being run as regional industrial policy as well as a change to how services are run.",
                "supersedes": ""
              }
            ],
            "statements": [
              {
                "cites": [
                  "L1",
                  "F55"
                ],
                "text": "Some context for the week: Andy Burnham has been Prime Minister since 20 July 2026, after Keir Starmer, and when he entered Downing Street he said he wanted to do politics differently."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S1:F2",
                  "S1:F7"
                ],
                "text": "The Ukraine thread opened with Washington's moves. The US and Russia reached an agreement on 9 October, and two days later President Trump announced a reciprocal energy ceasefire."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S2:F6",
                  "S6:F22",
                  "S7:F29",
                  "F30"
                ],
                "text": "Britain's answer was to push harder. On 12 October it brought more than 90% of Russia's oil producing capacity under UK sanctions. It is delivering £45 million of surface-to-air missiles, and on 14 October it set out a new package of Lightweight Multirole Missiles, with the two leaders discussing how to speed up defensive support before winter."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S6:F21",
                  "N1"
                ],
                "text": "With Ministers told to draw up further sanctions, military aid and fiscal packages within weeks, the pattern looks like London backing Kyiv separately from the US track rather than taking the ceasefire as a reason to ease off."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S4:F9",
                  "S4:F10",
                  "F14"
                ],
                "text": "The hybrid-threats work fits the same posture: a new partnership with Chancellor Merz and a National Centre for Information Defence. Both were set out in the same statement in which the Prime Minister thanked police and military personnel for their response to incidents at RAF Fairford and RAF Molesworth."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S13:F51",
                  "S14:F56",
                  "F57"
                ],
                "text": "At home, the Prime Minister's Scotland offer to work through No10 North and the Scotland Office came the same week that Holyrood agreed to match £10 million for two defence technical colleges. In Motherwell he also pointed to his choice of Falkirk-built buses for Greater Manchester."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N2"
                ],
                "text": "That college deal is real cooperation, but it is one project, not yet a new settlement on devolved powers."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S8:F31",
                  "F33",
                  "S9:F35",
                  "S11:F37"
                ],
                "text": "On rail, Derby got the GBR headquarters on a brownfield city-centre site, the first apprentice starters, and the £1 billion northern fleet to be built at Alstom's site."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N3",
                  "S10:F36"
                ],
                "text": "Putting all of these in one city suggests public ownership is also being used as regional industrial policy. Alongside that sits the fare freeze, which the government presents as a payoff of public ownership."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S5:F17"
                ],
                "text": "Next to watch: the UK-EU summit in Brussels on 20 November."
              }
            ],
            "stories": []
          }
        }
      ],
